$$\\begin{cases} 6x - 4y = 15 \\\\ kx + 10y = -8 \\end{cases}$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$-15$"},{"key":"B","text":"$-10$"},{"key":"C","text":"$10$"},{"key":"D","text":"$15$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'A system of two linear equations has no solution when the lines are parallel (same slope, different intercepts). Rewriting the first equation: $y = \\frac{6}{4}x - \\frac{15}{4} = \\frac{3}{2}x - \\frac{15}{4}$. The second equation: $y = -\\frac{k}{10}x - \\frac{8}{10}$. For parallel lines, the slopes must be equal: $-\\frac{k}{10} = \\frac{3}{2}$, so $k = -15$. The intercepts $-\\frac{15}{4}$ and $-\\frac{4}{5}$ are different, confirming the system is inconsistent. The value $-10$ comes from only partially setting up the slope comparison. The value $10$ results from a sign error when solving $-\\frac{k}{10} = \\frac{3}{2}$. The value $15$ reflects ignoring the negative sign entirely when equating slopes.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"sign_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);






































